From 93b6fe443b355088bf39844fe59c136f075a6c66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 15:55:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vo_gpu/cocoa: don't call -[NSWindow setStyleMask:] on 10.5
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

-[NSWindow setStyleMask:] is 10.6+; before that a window's style mask is
fixed at creation and the selector does not exist at all. MpvVideoWindow
overrides it and calls [super setStyleMask:], so on 10.5 every call raises
NSInvalidArgumentException:

  *** -[MpvVideoWindow setStyleMask:]: unrecognized selector sent to instance

(The runtime names the receiver's class, which is why our own override
appears in the message.) It aborts -setFullScreen: right after
_in_fullscreen = YES and before -setFrame:, the menubar hide and
-windowDidEnterFullScreen, so fullscreen does nothing and leaves
opts->fullscreen, _in_fullscreen and s->fullscreen three-way desynced --
which also permanently disables the -constrainFrameRect: clamp. The same
exception fires from -updateBorder: for runtime --border toggling. 10.6+
is unaffected; the api-floor inventory audited the style-mask constants but
not the setter, and COCOA-PORT-PLAN.md §4 actively prescribes it.

Probe the setter with +[NSWindow instancesRespondToSelector:] -- probing
self is useless, since this class overrides the selector and would always
answer YES -- and compile the probe out to a constant YES when the
deployment target is already 10.6, so 10.6+ builds keep the existing code
path verbatim. -setStyleMask: becomes a no-op below 10.6 rather than
raising; runtime border toggling is simply not expressible there (the mask
create_window() picks at startup is final), but --border=no still works.

Fullscreen gets a real fallback: -setLegacyFullScreen:onScreen: creates a
second, borderless MpvVideoWindow covering the target screen and moves the
view hierarchy -- and with it the NSOpenGLContext drawable -- into it via
the new -[MpvCocoaAdapter moveContentViewToWindow:], then moves it back on
the way out. s->window keeps pointing at the windowed window throughout;
only the view's parent changes. The destination window is ordered on screen
before the move, because -setView: on a view in an off-screen window leaves
the context with no drawable. s->view survives -removeFromSuperview on the
+1 it keeps from -alloc in create_ui(), and s->video auto-resizes with it.
The transition runs on the main thread with the VO thread blocked in
VOCTRL_VO_OPTS_CHANGED, so no frame is in flight.

-close tears the fullscreen window down first, so quitting while fullscreen
cannot leave a borderless window and a hidden menubar behind.
vo_cocoa_update_screen_info() now asks the window the view actually lives
in and ignores a nil -[NSWindow screen], which an ordered-out window
returns; on 10.6+ that is always s->window.

Untested on hardware: needs a 10.5 PPC run (fullscreen enter/exit, --fs at
startup, quit while fullscreen) and a 10.6 regression check.
